      "text": "To find the smallest positive integer that is both an integer power of 11 and is not a palindrome, we start by examining the powers of 11 and checking if they are palindromes.\n\nFirst, let's compute the first few powers of 11:\n\\[\n11^0 = 1\n\\]\n\\[\n11^1 = 11\n\\]\n\\[\n11^2 = 121\n\\]\n\\[\n11^3 = 1331\n\\]\n\\[\n11^4 = 14641\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to determine if any of these numbers are palindromes. A palindrome is a number that reads the same forwards and backwards.\n\n- \\(11^0 = 1\\) is a palindrome.\n- \\(11^1 = 11\\) is a palindrome.\n- \\(11^2 = 121\\) is a palindrome.\n- \\(11^3 = 1331\\) is not a palindrome.\n- \\(11^4 = 14641\\) is not a palindrome.\n\nAmong the powers of 11, we see that \\(11^3 = 1331\\) is the first number that is not a palindrome. Therefore, we need to check the next power, \\(11^4 = 14641\\), but we already know it is not a palindrome.\n\nThus, the smallest positive integer that is both an integer power of 11 and is not a palindrome is \\(\\boxed{1331}\\).",
